{
  "name": "familyrecipes",
  "main": "worker/index.ts",
  // Not secrets. The anon/publishable key is already served inside the JS bundle to every
  // visitor, and the Worker's whole security model is that it holds NOTHING more privileged
  // than a browser does: RLS decides what an anonymous caller may read.
  // They live here rather than in the dashboard on purpose: disconnecting the repo in Workers
  // Builds deletes the build configuration, vars included, and has done so on this project.
  "vars": {
    "SUPABASE_URL": "https://ghcclshgdtbystosfzjl.supabase.co",
    "SUPABASE_ANON_KEY": "sb_publishable_bViGBaGpqpV0tGKnghkgZQ_p-raLjKb"
  },
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-18",
  // RETIRED 2026-10-15. familyrecipes.aayus-pok.workers.dev served the whole family app as a
  // second, fully crawlable copy: its robots.txt said `Allow: /`, every page canonicalised to
  // ITSELF rather than to recipes.enamelvault.com, and it published its own sitemap listing
  // its own URLs. That is exactly the duplicate content the host split in worker/site.ts
  // exists to prevent, and it had been live the whole time.
  //
  // This host was previously kept because links sent to family pointed at it, and retiring it
  // breaks those links rather than redirecting them. That was the deliberate choice: a
  // redirect would have kept them alive, and it was weighed and declined.
  //
  // THE CUSTOM DOMAIN IS NOW THE ONLY WAY IN. There is no fallback URL if it ever breaks, so
  // re-enabling this temporarily is the escape hatch worth remembering during an incident.
  "workers_dev": false,
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist",
    "not_found_handling": "single-page-application",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    // WITHOUT THIS, THE WORKER NEVER RUNS FOR `/`. Cloudflare serves any request matching a
    // real file in dist/ straight from its asset store, skipping the Worker, so the security
    // headers reached /help and /sitemap.xml (no matching file, so they fell through) and
    // never reached the home page, which is index.html.
    //
    // MUST BE `true`, NOT A LIST OF ROUTES. An array means "run the Worker first ONLY for
    // these", and every other path is then handled by the asset layer alone: not_found_handling
    // serves index.html directly without ever invoking the Worker. Setting ["/", "/index.html"]
    // was tried on 2026-10-02 and broke production in two ways within a minute, because
    // /sitemap.xml and /recipes/:id are Worker routes with no matching file. The sitemap
    // started returning the SPA shell as text/html, and every shared recipe link lost its
    // per-recipe OpenGraph tags and fell back to the generic card. Both are silent failures:
    // the pages still answer 200.
    //
    // The cost of `true` is that a Worker exception now takes the static assets down with it,
    // where before they would still have served. That is the trade, and it is the right way
    // round: the Worker is already the only thing that answers for every route except the
    // handful of real files, so almost nothing was independent of it anyway.
    // NO AUTOMATIC `.html` RESOLUTION, AND THIS IS A BUG FIX RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
    //
    // The default ("auto-trailing-slash") serves `kitchen.html` for the path `/kitchen`, and
    // this repo ships three HTML entries at the root: index.html, kitchen.html, admin.html.
    // So on recipes.enamelvault.com, measured on production 2026-10-26:
    //
    //     /kitchen -> <title>Enamel Vault for kitchens</title>
    //     /admin   -> <title>Enamel Vault console</title>
    //     /site    -> the marketing TEMPLATE, rack placeholder and all
    //
    // `/kitchen` is a real route in the family app, the "My Kitchen" link in its own nav. A
    // cook who reloaded that page, opened it in a new tab or used a bookmark was handed the
    // professional kitchen app, which then said "That page is not here". Clicking the link
    // worked, because react-router never asks the server, which is exactly why this survived:
    // it is invisible to every click-through test and to the whole unit suite.
    //
    // route() already refuses `/kitchen.html` and `/site.html` as paths. That was the right
    // instinct and it was not enough: it never saw the extensionless form the asset layer
    // invented. With "none" an unmatched path falls to not_found_handling, which is the SPA
    // shell for the host the Worker is serving, and the Worker's own internal fetches name
    // the entries exactly, so they still resolve.
    "html_handling": "none",
    "run_worker_first": true
  }
}
